		<method name="evaluate" qualifiers="static">
			<return type="NetwPredictJudgement" />
			<param index="0" name="domain" type="int" />
			<param index="1" name="verdict" type="int" />
			<param index="2" name="predicted" type="Dictionary" />
			<param index="3" name="payload" type="Dictionary" />
			<param index="4" name="wiring" type="Dictionary" />
			<param index="5" name="field_sink" type="Dictionary" />
			<description>
				Judges [param predicted] against the acknowledged [param payload], by the exact [param verdict] when [param domain] claimed reproducibility and by [param wiring]'s tolerances otherwise. [param field_sink] is cleared and refilled with the per-property error on every branch, as an argument rather than a return value so a caller already holding the dictionary keeps its reference.
			</description>
		</method>
		<method name="topology_fingerprint" qualifiers="static">
			<return type="int" />
			<param index="0" name="facts" type="Dictionary" />
			<param index="1" name="quantum" type="int" />
			<description>
				[method fact_fingerprint] over [param facts] with [param quantum] folded in under the key [code skip-lint]quantum[/code], and nothing else.
				The quantum is HOW MANY physics steps a drive actually bought, and it belongs in the topology hash because the physics server integrates on its own cadence: a drive and a solve are two events, and only their ratio makes a compared transition mean the same thing on both peers. Without it the boundary ladder walks to CLOSURE and reports that every antecedent agreed.
				A different [param quantum] is therefore a different topology, deliberately.
			</description>
		</method>
